well Andrew McPhillips is chief  economist at the Yorkshire Building  Society jean-marc VanDerveer I used to  head up the residential arm of the  commercial property developer British  land and it's now chief executive of the  rental housing provide a platform and  Stewart Baisley the executive chairman  of Home Builders Federation is in our  central London studio Andrew let me  start with you I mean apart from London  is there genuinely a housing shortage  around the country I mean after all  household formation over the last few  years has fallen well short of  government predictions about 1.2 million  in fact I think if we look at  affordability we can see that that's  getting stretched right across the  country and it's about delivering  quality homes where people want to live  that are affordable whether that's to  purchase or indeed to rent but the how  the housing supply and demand factors  here isn't the big fattie what's really  stoked up to mind it's not so much a  supply-side crisis as the fact that  we've had ultra low interest rates for  nine years and that's just stoked up  demand yeah there has been an effect  from the interest rate policy and and  policies such as help to buy there  certainly increased on the demand side I  think it's fair to say we haven't seen  as big a response from the supply side  as one would have hopes given that you  know increase in demand so what have you  made of today you are quite impressed or  I think there are some warm words in  there to welcome I think particularly  looking at local authorities being given  more power to get involved in delivering  housing projects but I think as always  with these policies it's a case of it  actually being followed through with  action on the ground you're Maki you  operate in the rental sets you're a big  rental provider right it looks like the  government after years of talking about  home ownership is talking about rental  in a serious way you encouraged by that  yes we are we take the white paper as  very good news because the previous  government was focusing solely on the  home ownership and and the housing issue  is a massive issue and it cannot be  tackled only in one way it needs to be  tackled in different ways and covering  the different tenure social housing  private rented and the home ownership as  well but so far the previous years  people who have been left in the middle  who cannot afford or don't want to buy  yet and are not benefiting from the  social housing have been left on the  side  as there hasn't been any proper product  developed for their needs because out of  out of the total market basically 98% of  the houses for rent are managed by  individual angles not professional  manuals and there is the need for  product and the government does  understand that and they are providing  support for new companies like ours to  be able to develop those products okay  what Stewart basically in Westminster  we've heard loads of government attempts  to remedy the housing market and pep up  building over the years is there  anything genuinely new in this white  paper as far as you're concerned what  it's building on some of the policies  that have been around for the last five  or six years you're quite right we have  heard a lot about housing because people  like me and others have been talking  about a housing crisis now for the best  part of a decade or more and I think  it's fair to say that this white paper  today it's a hundred pages deep it's  there's a lot for us all to absorb in it  and much of it is to be the subject of  consultation of course none of this  necessarily will make a huge difference  in the immediate short-term but there is  a genuine attempt here to get to grips  with the root cause of the problem and  as your other guests have mentioned to  recognize that we need to be providing  housing homes for people across the  country of all 10 years some for sale  for for buyers to buy in the traditional  way but for shared ownership and for  rent and for private rent as well as for  social rent at its heart it's also  tackling one of the biggest issues which  holds back development in our country  and that is the lack of a properly  structured planning process at local  authority level and there are a lot of  measures in the white paper which are  aimed at addressing that white paper  recognizes for example that some 40  percent of local authorities still don't  have a local plan in place and clearly  if you don't have a local plan then you  are planning to fail in terms of housing  deliveries so there is a lot to be  applauded in the white paper today  particularly in the way that it's going  about asking local authorities to work  with providers whether they be  developers or builders of homes for rent  or anybody else involved in the housing  space in making sure that the local  needs are robustly scientifically  so that each local authority has a very  good accurate idea of what the housing  needs in its locality going to be and  then measuring the delivery of those  homes across the five-year period of the  vehicle plan on an annual basis and  ensuring that sites are made available  for all sorts of developments not just  large developments but also developments  for small developers for retirement  developers for builders of homes for  rent and so on and so forth so that what  about your sector Stewart's  I mean one of the big things that's come  out of this today's it's something like  60% of prizes private housing is built  by just 10 firms now the government is  attempting to get more SMEs involved in  in-house building it's like are they  gonna succeed  well they've as I said they put in place  some ideas and some policies  particularly in how the local plans are  put in place which will help to redress  the decline in small and medium sized  enterprises which is really what has  been at the heart of this if you can  trace this back in a prepare ever  appeared to 25 years you know this  housing crisis is not something that's  just happened in the last five or six  years and it's not really happened as a  result of the economic recession it's  happened as a result of a succession of  policies which have failed to ensure  that we've been building enough heads  for a quarter of a century or more and  one of the effects of those policies has  been a dramatic decline in a number of  small and medium sized builders which we  define as builders producing a hundred  hands a year or less there were 12 and a  half thousand of those 25 years ago  today there are just two and a half  thousand of those and we have seen  dramatic growth in housing output over  the last three or four years which is  often overlooked when people talk about  what's actually happening in the housing  market housing numbers the number of  homes built has increased by 50% in the  last three years but pretty much all of  that growth has come from those larger  companies that you mentioned because  they're better financed and able to take  advantage of the ability to invest in  land and  and grow their businesses okay smaller  builders have struggled with both the  red tape and the regulation of the  planning system which all builders have  to grapple with but it's particularly  tough when you're a small Kenda with our  huge resources and they've also  struggled to gain development finance to  enable them and to invest in land and  grow their businesses jamort you were  nodding at some of Stuart's comments  about local authorities how receptive  are local councils I mean clearly the  government tried a few years ago to get  them to think more about planning needs  there was he haven't done so so planning  I think they've done some work but I  think they are not going fast enough  it's not moving fast enough and I think  in the white paper what is being  provided is additional resources to make  sure that local authority of the  capacity and the capabilities to to make  sure that the planning concerns are  being provided faster and sooner because  you know it would be more if we can  build faster as well that will help to  solve the housing crisis and there is  also an attempt for the government to  help educate the local authorities to  develop and promote as well the rental  product as well because this is really a  tenure that has been left on the side  for so many years and the local  authorities don't necessarily apart from  social housing understand very well that  tenure and the government is willing  really to support that tenure and help  them to understand it better and educate  it and I'm sure one of the things that a  lot of people talked about today but  didn't really appear that much in the  white paper was stamp duty it clearly is  something I mean the rental providers  dislike it private landlords find it an  issue now particularly since Osborne  jacked up per stamp duty on rental  properties you actually wanted to see  stamp duty switched from from buyers to  sellers yeah we were looking at kind of  flipping it around so you get rid of a  first-time buyer ever having to stump up  front cost somewhere like London it's a  significant barrier to getting on the  property ladder for an average  first-time buyer that we lent to they'd  be looking at over ten thousand pounds  as an an upfront bill so switching it to  the seller they've got the cash from the  proceeds that their sale to pay it more  easily at the top of the market that was  talked about downsizing and perhaps some  support their elderly homeowners from  downsizing yeah and I think what we were  looking at you could introduce some  farmers exemption for people that were  looking to downsize if that's what they  want to do it would  them move into a smaller property that  suited to their needs if they want to do  that right Andrew Phillips John Marvin  Aviva answer your Baisley thanks all  thank you

